# Citation discipline — no unsourced constants, ever

This node is hiked into the woods and left for months. Every magic number in it —
a register address, a baud rate, a timeout, an RX window, a sleep current — is a claim
about physical reality. **If a claim has no source, it is a guess, and guesses are how
you brick a node you cannot reach.**

`AGENTS.md` already forbids inventing pinouts and Modbus maps. This rule makes that
enforceable.

## The `CITE` format

Citations are structured comments so `scripts/check_citations.py` can verify them.
One per line, immediately above or beside the thing being justified:

```c
// CITE(datasheet): RAK5802 RS-485 IO module pinout — https://docs.rakwireless.com/...
// CITE(spec): MODBUS over Serial Line v1.02 §2.5.1.1, 3.5-char frame gap — https://modbus.org/...
// CITE(prior-art): beegee-tokyo/WisBlock-API-V2 sleep pattern — https://github.com/beegee-tokyo/WisBlock-API-V2
static const uint32_t MODBUS_FRAME_GAP_US = 4010;  // 3.5 chars @ 4800 8N1
```

In markdown, an inline link with the same category prefix satisfies the check:
`[CITE(datasheet): RAK4631 datasheet](https://docs.rakwireless.com/...)`.

### Categories

| Category | Means | Examples |
|---|---|---|
| `datasheet` | Manufacturer / vendor primary doc | RAKwireless docs, Nordic nRF52840, Semtech SX1262, RK900-09 manual |
| `spec` | Normative standard | LoRa Alliance RP002 / Link Layer, Modbus.org, FCC 47 CFR 15.247, Cayenne LPP |
| `policy` | Network operator rule | TTN Fair Use Policy, TTN frequency plans |
| `prior-art` | Another implementation, ours or public | `beegee-tokyo/*`, `meshtastic/firmware`, repos in `docs/LIBRARIES.md` |
| `sibling` | Local companion repo | `forest-weather-machines`, `rak-4-5-wire` — cite path **and** commit SHA |
| `bench` | Our own measured evidence | An entry in `docs/EVIDENCE.md` — never a claim, always a measurement |

All URLs go in `docs/CITATIONS.md` as the canonical registry. Cite the registry key plus
the URL; do not paste a bare link with no context about what it establishes.

## Minimum bar per change

| Change type | Requirement |
|---|---|
| Any firmware behavior change | **≥ 3 citations across ≥ 2 categories** |
| Any register, address, baud, slave ID, channel/type ID | `datasheet` **or** `spec`, on the same or adjacent line — no exceptions |
| Any timing, timeout, retry, backoff, or interval | `spec` or `policy`, plus `prior-art` where one exists |
| Any power/sleep/current claim | `datasheet` for the figure, `bench` once measured |
| Payload encode/decode | `spec` (Cayenne LPP) **and** `sibling` (the live TTN decoder) |
| Docs describing behavior | Same bar as the code it documents |
| Pure refactor, typo, formatting | No new citations required; must not delete existing ones |

**Prior art is not authority.** A GitHub project shows that something works in practice; it
does not establish that it is correct. Never let `prior-art` stand alone behind a register
map, a timing value, or an RF parameter — pair it with `datasheet` or `spec`. When prior
art and the datasheet disagree, the datasheet wins and the disagreement gets written down.

## Verify, don't recall

- **Fetch the URL before citing it.** Do not cite from memory. Dead or hallucinated links
  are worse than no citation, because they look authoritative in review. During setup,
  five of the URLs written from memory turned out to be 404s — including two that other
  published specs cite. The corrections log at the end of `docs/CITATIONS.md` records them.
- **Never commit an unverified entry.** Verify it or leave it out; `check_citations.py`
  fails the build on `UNVERIFIED`. A registry that ships warnings teaches everyone to
  ignore warnings, and then the one that matters gets ignored too.
- Cite the **specific section or page**, not a doc hub homepage.
- Record what the source actually establishes, in your own words, next to the citation.
- If a source is genuinely unreachable (bot-blocked, login-walled), find a mirror or an
  equivalent authority rather than committing a broken row — eCFR blocks automation, so
  FCC 15.247 is cited via Cornell LII.
- Local `sibling` citations pin a commit SHA — those repos move independently and an
  unpinned path is not reproducible.

## When sources conflict

Do not silently pick one. Record the conflict in `docs/decisions/` as an ADR with both
citations, state which one the firmware follows and why, and flag it for the user.
Two known live conflicts are already logged in ADR-0002 — read it before touching the
battery current sign or the payload channel map.
